WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.060
Marvel's shows on Disney+ are set to shake
up the way superhero stories can be told on-screen.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.120
Here's why these series might be different
from previous Marvel TV offerings and how

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.780
all these exciting series are going to blow
you away!

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.560
Beginning with Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D. in 2013,
which centered around Clark Gregg's character

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.050
from The Avengers, there have been a number
of television shows that have spun off of

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.240
characters or events from the movies, making
them officially part of the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:24.760
Universe.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.160
However, even the Marvel shows with the closest
ties to the primary MCU such as Agent Carter,

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:34.480
which focused on Hayley Atwell's popular character
from Captain America: The First Avenger still

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.560
weren't considered part of Marvel's "phases."

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:39.960
"What'll it be?"

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.640
"Angie what are you doing here?"

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.360
"Bustin' my hump, working a double.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.400
What about you?

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.400
Shouldn't you be working, English?"

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.880
The MCU has had three "phases" so far.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.920
The first began with Iron Man and concluded
with The Avengers, the second spanned from

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.789
Iron Man 3 to Ant-Man, and included 2015's
Avengers: Age of Ultron, and the third began

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:01.130
with Captain America: Civil War and concluded
with Spider-Man: Far From Home, which served

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:03.520
as a coda to the record-shattering Avengers:
Endgame.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.820
However, Phase Four will include not only
five new films but also five new television

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:12.510
shows, making this the first time ever that
TV shows will be considered an integral part

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.380
of the stories being told by the films.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.500
Marvel's Disney+ shows won't be the first
television series to pull in characters who

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.030
were first seen on the big screen.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.250
Peggy Carter and Phil Coulson in Agent Carter
and Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D. are the most obvious

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.840
examples of film characters making the leap
to television, but Nick Fury, Maria Hill,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.900
Lady Sif, and Howard Stark have also all appeared
in Marvel's TV series.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.520
"Nobody cares about the long game."

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:38.520
"Damn!

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:41.700
What am I doing wrong, Jarvis?"

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.140
But pulling in a supporting film character
for a guest arc on a TV series is a far cry

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.500
from what Marvel will be doing with its inaugural
crop of Disney+ shows, which will center not

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.150
on side characters from the films, but on
actual Avengers like Hawkeye, Scarlet Witch,

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.020
and the Winter Soldier.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.180
Recognizable faces from the MCU aren't an
occasional perk for the Disney+ series.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.920
Instead, they're the main attraction.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.710
By building series around characters who've
already had significant arcs play out over

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:10.500
multiple films, the first five Disney+ shows
will not only be able to jump right into meaty

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.060
storylines, skipping over introductions and
origin stories, but they'll also be able to

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.660
draw on the audience's existing attachment
to these characters instead of needing to

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.080
take time to build up affection.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.340
When discussing the Disney+ Marvel shows,
Marvel Studios president and MCU mastermind

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:25.840
Kevin Feige told Screen Rant:

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.080
"We want to do something that's going to stand
right alongside the MCU films and will be

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.920
completely intertwined, and the story and
the characters will go back and forth between

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.820
Disney+ programming and the films."

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.800
This is exciting for a number of reasons.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.080
Not only can we look forward to storylines
that will jump between the series and the

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.252
films, making it a lot more rewarding to be
a fan of both, but the Disney+ shows are already

00:02:45.252 --> 00:02:49.340
set to introduce some popular Marvel Comics
characters who fans have long wanted to see

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:50.340
appear on the big screen.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.260
“Whatever you do Jen, try not to punch Fandral."

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.740
"The tall, skinny guy?

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.360
Why would I want to punch..."

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.580
"Well, hello tall, green and..."

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:04.140
Already announced are Kate Bishop, who will
appear alongside Clint Barton in Hawkeye,

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.960
and Monica Rambeau a.k.a. Spectrum, who will
co-star in WandaVision.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.120
Then there are brand new characters like Ms.
Marvel, She-Hulk, and Moon Knight, who will

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.500
each headline their own series.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.240
That's sure to be just the tip of the iceberg
when it comes to Marvel's extensive roster

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.060
of characters and which ones they aim to bring
to the screen with their own Disney+ shows.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.440
In the past, when Marvel shows have been made,
they've been produced by Marvel Television

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:30.620
and overseen by executive producer Jeph Loeb,
who's headed up Marvel's TV branch since 2010.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:34.700
While some of these shows, such as Jessica
Jones and Legion, were both popular among

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.480
audiences and well-received by critics, they've
all existed mostly independently from the

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.100
films, which are all produced by Marvel Studios
and overseen by Kevin Feige.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.760
With two different captains at the helm of
their respective ships, Marvel Television

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.670
and Marvel Studios each charted their own
courses, which occasionally ran parallel to

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.400
one another but rarely intersected.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.500
" … all around me, you people were raining
down hell."

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.700
"So go after the big, green guy or the flag
waver.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:03:59.820
I wasn't even there!"

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.000
With the arrival of the Disney+ shows, however,
all that changes.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:07.320
Now, Kevin Feige is calling the shots for
both the films and the TV series that make

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.460
up Phase Four and beyond.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:12.660
This means that instead of an independent
movieverse and a mostly unrelated TV world,

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.460
both the shows and the series will be folded
into a single cohesive narrative for the first

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:16.960
time.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:21.100
If the previous films are any indication,
this means we can likely expect characters

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:25.080
from one show or film to unexpectedly pop
up in another, storylines to begin in one

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.100
property and finish in a different one, and
for clues for what's coming next in the MCU

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.600
to be sprinkled throughout everything.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.640
With the arrival of the Disney+ Marvel shows,
beginning in the fall of 2020 with The Falcon

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.740
and the Winter Soldier, Kevin Feige has promised
"new and continuing stories" which, quote,

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.500
"will be on the same level of quality that
you've come to expect from Marvel Studios."

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:48.100
Further, Feige has said that, quote, "these
will be major storylines set in the MCU with

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.250
ramifications that will be felt both through
the other Disney+ series we're producing and

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.580
our features on the big screen."

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.250
From the way Feige is speaking, it sounds
as though Marvel Studios will be taking the

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:01.449
exact same approach to their Disney+ shows
as they have with their MCU films, weaving

00:05:01.449 --> 00:05:03.720
them all around one overarching central narrative.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:07.000
However, while Marvel's films have to get
through their storylines in a little over

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.940
two hours each, the shows will have six or
more hours in which to explore their stories.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.500
That means that the TV series can take the
time to delve into some of the longer or more

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:19.030
complex narratives that may have been impossible
to condense into a single film like the relationship

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:23.050
between Wanda Maximoff and the Vision, for
example and can afford to finally devote a

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:27.010
significant amount of time to developing characters
who've previously had to make the most of

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.260
a handful of scenes spread across multiple
films.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.340
Yup, we're talking about these two:

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:33.420
"I hate you."

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.260
Unlike previous Marvel TV series, which, like
most television shows, have been fairly episodic

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:42.760
in nature, Elizabeth Olsen and Paul Bettany
told MTV that WandaVision will be more like

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.230
a six-hour movie than a conventional TV series.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:49.480
Or, actually… if Bettany's description of
the script is anything to go off of, maybe

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.280
"conventional" isn't the right word for it.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:52.420
He further dished:

00:05:52.740 --> 00:06:00.760
"It's so avant-garde and weird and messed
up, and then moves seamlessly into more familiar

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.400
territory … "

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:05.500
With the majority of the other live-action
Disney+ series also reported to come in at

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:09.460
six to eight episodes per season and the TV
series said to directly impact the world of

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.540
the films it seems safe to assume that WandaVision
won't be the only show that feels more like

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.040
a movie.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.860
If you're trying to picture what a six-hour
MCU story might look like, just think about

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.520
all the ground that gets covered between Avengers:
Infinity War and Avengers: Endgame.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:26.199
Now, imagine that instead of two three-hour
movies, the Infinity Saga was one six-hour

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:27.920
show, and marvel at the possibilities.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.640
Nice to see you're still as pleasant as you
ever were."

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.220
"And it's just as nice to see as you're still
bulletproof, Stark."

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.889
Already, the showrunners for the Disney+ series
are excited about the possibilities that a

00:06:41.889 --> 00:06:43.410
new medium will allow them to explore.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.780
Jac Schaeffer, the showrunner for WandaVision,
admitted:

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.640
"We have these big, meaty questions that we
get to spend more than two and a half hours

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:50.140
exploring.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:54.920
So that's been the work, is making it exciting
and intriguing and head-turning at every moment."

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:59.290
Captain Marvel producer Mary Livanos seemed
to agree with Schaeffer on the expansive scope

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:03.580
of the Disney+ shows, telling ComicBook.com
that the television format will allow Marvel's

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:08.100
shows to tell the equivalent of a full comic
book-style event, or multi-issue story.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:12.480
Typically in comics, one storyline following
a single central character or group can play

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:17.320
out over multiple issues, each one filled
with twists, surprises, and plenty of action

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:18.000
and emotion.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:22.060
It sounds as though the Marvel team is eager
to bring this sort of focused, extended storytelling

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:26.970
to the MCU, with the Disney+ shows allowing
them to present satisfying, character-driven

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.530
stories in their entirety instead of parceled
out one piece at a time over the course of

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:31.220
several movies.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.360
Marvel has already announced that it will
be using the Disney+ television format to

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.310
bring some fan favorites into the MCU fold;
however, there are some other characters from

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:42.260
Marvel Comics who may actually fare better
on TV than film.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.169
Take, for example, teams such as the X-Men
and the Fantastic Four, who've previously

00:07:46.169 --> 00:07:48.680
faltered under the two-hour time constraint
of a film.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:53.191
But with the Disney+ series set to seamlessly
transition characters from film to TV, and

00:07:53.191 --> 00:07:57.090
vice versa, the streaming platform may be
the perfect place for some of Marvel's most

00:07:57.090 --> 00:08:00.350
iconic teams to make their debut before jumping
into the films.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:04.290
It may also be exactly the right venue to
introduce individual members of a group over

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:08.330
several different shows before teaming them
up for a film or a Disney+ series of their

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:08.820
own.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:13.620
The first two phases of the MCU consisted
of six films each, while the third contained

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:17.910
a whopping 11, three of which were "event"
films which brought a wide group of characters

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.940
together from across multiple solo films.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:24.280
This has meant that Marvel has typically been
spending between eight to ten hours on stories

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:29.370
bridging the gaps between event films, introducing
and developing characters, laying plot groundwork,

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.940
and tightening the links between its multiple
ongoing storylines.

00:08:32.089 --> 00:08:35.980
Previously, it has taken Marvel around three
to four years to get through these ten hours

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:40.240
needed to set up an event film, due to the
limitations of only releasing two to three

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.240
movies per year.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.340
But with the introduction of the Disney+ shows,
Marvel will now be able to get through 30

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.260
or more hours of story every year, as opposed
to its previous four to six.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:53.379
Such a massive increase in how much storytelling
Marvel can fit into a single year may allow

00:08:53.379 --> 00:08:56.100
them to lay the groundwork for bigger stories
even faster.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.000
Now, instead of ten years building up to the
next Endgame-level event, we may get there

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.000
in half the time.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.220
Although Marvel Studios has been dabbling
in a number of genres for quite some time

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.119
touting Captain America: The Winter Soldier
as a political thriller, Ant-Man as a comedic

00:09:09.119 --> 00:09:13.399
heist film, Spider-Man: Homecoming as a John
Hughes-esque high school comedy, and the upcoming

00:09:13.399 --> 00:09:17.190
Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness
as a horror film there are still some genres

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:18.700
Marvel has yet to explore.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.060
Television opens up the realm of possibilities,
not just for which characters Marvel can introduce

00:09:23.069 --> 00:09:25.720
and which stories they can tell, but how they
can tell them.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:30.140
For example, WandaVision will purportedly
be a "sitcom," a genre which only exists in

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:30.640
television.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:35.040
The teaser shown at D23 seemed to indicate
that the series would take significant influence

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.020
from The Dick Van Dyke Show, one of the most
classic examples of the sitcom format.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:42.639
And while we don't yet know how long each
episode of the Jeffrey Wright-narrated What

00:09:42.639 --> 00:09:47.160
If…? will be, its animated format and self-contained
nature calls to mind the animated shows of

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:51.249
the '80s and '90s, including Marvel's own
X-Men: The Animated Series, which had its

00:09:51.249 --> 00:09:55.900
characters facing a different threat nearly
every week and largely resisted serialization.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:59.260
There's no telling what other genres Marvel
will be able to explore through their Disney+

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:03.639
series even the ones that have already been
announced are not genres anyone would've anticipated

00:10:03.639 --> 00:10:07.940
a couple years ago but if the first three
phases of the MCU are any indication, we've

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.680
only just begun to scratch the surface.

